WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.740
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:01.740 --> 00:00:03.510
TOM CONNER: Hi,
I'm Dr. Tom Conner.

00:00:03.510 --> 00:00:07.060
I'm the William P. Harris
Professor of Military History

00:00:07.060 --> 00:00:09.650
at Hillsdale College.

00:00:09.650 --> 00:00:12.910
I came to Hillsdale in 1983,
which was the same year I've

00:00:12.910 --> 00:00:14.860
finished my PhD.

00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:18.020
And I came here because
they offered me a job.

00:00:18.020 --> 00:00:21.190
I've often told people
that my coming to Hillsdale

00:00:21.190 --> 00:00:23.410
was part of an arranged
marriage but it quickly

00:00:23.410 --> 00:00:25.450
grew into a love affair.

00:00:25.450 --> 00:00:29.530
My doctorate in history was
in the modern European field

00:00:29.530 --> 00:00:32.380
with an emphasis in
diplomatic history.

00:00:32.380 --> 00:00:35.140
I've since kind of grown
that into a kind of a hobby

00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:36.550
field in military history.

00:00:36.550 --> 00:00:38.710
I was not trained as
a military historian,

00:00:38.710 --> 00:00:42.470
but I was given this chair
about 10 or 12 years ago.

00:00:42.470 --> 00:00:43.690
I've always liked history.

00:00:43.690 --> 00:00:47.800
I grew up in a household where
my dad was a World War II

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:50.800
veteran and had a high
school degree only.

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:53.860
Like a lot of World War II vets,
I think once they got home,

00:00:53.860 --> 00:00:58.630
he made it his ambition
to read about the war.

00:00:58.630 --> 00:01:01.700
He and my mother would do the
chores after dinner at night,

00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:04.849
and he'd be reading in his
easy chair for hours on end.

00:01:04.849 --> 00:01:09.520
And that, combined with I was
very lucky in school always

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:12.490
to have had good teachers
and I just kind of developed

00:01:12.490 --> 00:01:16.060
a love for history, I think
partly because my father

00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:18.090
modeled it to me.

00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:21.760
A liberal arts education
brings a person more fully

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:25.900
into contact with the whole
wide range of subjects that had

00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:29.640
to do with our common humanity.

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:32.230
One of the best definitions
I've ever heard of history

00:01:32.230 --> 00:01:35.050
is it's philosophy
teaching by example.

00:01:35.050 --> 00:01:37.390
In the historical record,
in the study of history,

00:01:37.390 --> 00:01:40.090
one can see just
about every dimension

00:01:40.090 --> 00:01:45.340
of the humanity of each one
of us across the generations.

00:01:45.340 --> 00:01:47.305
I know I'm a better
person because of the time

00:01:47.305 --> 00:01:50.590
that I've spent here, the
students and my faculty

00:01:50.590 --> 00:01:51.670
colleagues alike.

00:01:51.670 --> 00:01:54.080
I mean, it's really
a community effort.

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:57.370
I think we try to pick each
other up, encourage each other.

00:01:57.370 --> 00:02:00.670
Dr. Arnn likes to say
that everything we do here

00:02:00.670 --> 00:02:03.370
is out of friendship.

00:02:03.370 --> 00:02:07.250
And I've really not
only enjoyed that,

00:02:07.250 --> 00:02:10.320
but I know I've been
enriched by it as well.

